{"title":"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_9\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 9</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(bounding)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (bounding)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(complementary_counting)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (complementary counting)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(constructive_counting)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (constructive counting)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:35)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:35)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_(Fast_and_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 (Fast and Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_Sohil_Rathi\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by Sohil Rathi</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_9\">Problem 9</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 9\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many ordered triples of integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/b/31b1e1af4531148cbc079023f68c2b0102c4ee69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, y, z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" /> satisfy the following system of inequalities?\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/1/6d121518e48fb49d5fca6d97d4b409d11fe77458.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} -x-y-z&amp;\\leq -2\\\\ -x+y+z&amp;\\leq 2\\\\ x-y+z&amp;\\leq 2\\\\ x+y-z&amp;\\leq 2 \\end{align*}\" width=\"133\" height=\"90\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/6/29681114ce795b3ce3254e76082cc4af4f5fc563.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 4 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 8 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 11 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 15 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 17 \\qquad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"412\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28bounding.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(bounding)\">Solution 1 (bounding)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (bounding)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>All equations are homogeneous linearly and symmetric. Therefore, we can approach via bounding.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/2/8b288c90b3465921d0abd9314baa35558cbcefad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x+y+z \\ge 2\\]\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/a/19ad4f21018df40ce98e9ada973d504b7b3210a4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[-x+y+z \\le 2\\]\" width=\"119\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/6/37638ca87a931ec76c5f4b00cda009a11dfffe4e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x-y+z \\le 2\\]\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/7/84766c93ceaa2fdb0621f8be19a98cf92cdd1afc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x+y-z \\le 2\\]\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>We need to bound each of the inequalities. Begin by bounding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/4/44417a4c5992694f5cd0088fb183d70b87763282.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z \\le 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" />. Then, bound <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/b/59b63f18454186ccacae3817877b8a7cf498c8da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-x-y-z -x+y+z \\le 0 \\implies x \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"312\" height=\"17\" />. Finally, bound <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/4/8a41191a86760acbbe2d95874e1bf76bffc56f22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\le 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />. By symmetry, one can find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/0/e20de731c4acfc20ab985d255a65c90a406cf218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,z \\ge 0, \\le 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"117\" height=\"17\" />. We must have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/e/21ef381457bbbf2691d0610a614b6f7d98c3dc0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z \\ge 2, \\le 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"145\" height=\"17\" />. Approach via minimalism.\n</p><p>If the minimum of the three integers is 0, WLOG say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, then we require <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/a/1aab714e59d1249ed2c4c29dd55d70e493bee7f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y+z \\ge 2, \\le 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"17\" />. That implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/b/2bb7e37f28426e2420930275e59e6e8ec86b01d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 2, z = 0; y=1, z =1, z = 0, y=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"299\" height=\"17\" /> from which 3 cases arise. Due to symmetry, the cases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/a/57a0f6a311e08620b10d10e1ef9eeaf236ebe1af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,2), (2,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"91\" height=\"20\" /> are redundant, as I can let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa9f0d97d2f39f78f05e05da40bf04f5a7c0726c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and obtain a similar result. Therefore, we have 2 viable cases in which 3 permutations so 6 total scenarios.\n</p><p>If the minimum of the three is 1, WLOG say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, then we require <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/1/a31b788591fc6fe351b46ceff56e360113ed7a4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y+z \\ge 1, \\le 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"17\" />. That implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/2/a920a516dace4bbebd3f530e3b096cc5e773b7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 2, z = 1; y =1. z=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"194\" height=\"17\" />, which gives 3 and 1 case respectively, from which 4 cases.\n</p><p>If the minimum of the three is 2, WLOG say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, then we require <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/e/4ee97d3beb242b5d1acae762e415930070fa94db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y+z \\ge 0, \\le 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"17\" /> in which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/2/342624cb6b84b22f8b95351381aa0700d7ec2857.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=2, z=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"17\" /> is the only solution, giving 1 case.\n</p><p>The result is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/6/266a41e187b77416ec197182a1d10e66dfb40ef7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6+4+1 = \\boxed{11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"127\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28complementary_counting.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(complementary_counting)\">Solution 2 (complementary counting)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (complementary counting)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>From Solution 1, we know that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/e/45eed01251de924b773ed9b098923bae5db089d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[0 \\leq x, y, z \\leq 2,\\]\" width=\"115\" height=\"15\" />\nso since each variable has to be an integer, the number of possible ordered triples <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/b/31b1e1af4531148cbc079023f68c2b0102c4ee69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, y, z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" /> with no other restrictions is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/4/0a4cab0e2e4cb14f95a8217edd2ed7d48e0aa8f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^3 = 27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"17\" />. Therefore, to find the number of solutions of the given system of inequalities, we count the number of ordered triples that are <i>not</i> solutions of the system and subtract these from the total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/5/1051e7d41ec0b8bd688a872be17538413f4ee97b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nSimplifying the first inequality, we obtain\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/e/c4e547d253c4fa31d59e2df8e25448c9f46e095e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x+y+z \\geq 2,\\]\" width=\"110\" height=\"15\" />\nwhich is false (under the conditions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/1/471e7a49022ce27a977d572b61e14798e29a90b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq x,y,z \\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"17\" /> that we already have from above) only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/6/786294ba81c75be655bda0e12c983b0ef8919a1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. In the former case, there is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possible ordered triple (namely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/b/04b2f03235e96f5ca52af029dba7a008ee6f71b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z) = (0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"143\" height=\"20\" />), while in the latter case, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possibilities (corresponding to each of the variables being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> while the others are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />). Hence there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/cca0e40dbe6b9d8507944ac92eb83b4fa405d4e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+3 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> possible triples that make this inequality false.\n</p><p>Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/1/471e7a49022ce27a977d572b61e14798e29a90b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq x,y,z \\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"17\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/6/3a68debfc38f751b37cf686fa78ebd4a00817a2e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[-x+y+z \\leq 0+2+2 = 4,\\]\" width=\"219\" height=\"15\" /> and so the second inequality of the system would be false only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/8/03861df1a116f433c21a2723bf08c56e2ac48aa0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-x+y+z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"16\" /> is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/2/582221914474aedcf56e365600406f21da611f6c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y+z \\geq 3+x \\geq 3,\\]\" width=\"143\" height=\"15\" /> giving two possible cases: \n</p><p>Case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/e/11efa886f019143fd7cfcd72c12d5f883a1f832a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = z = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>In this case, to ensure that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/8/03861df1a116f433c21a2723bf08c56e2ac48aa0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-x+y+z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"16\" /> is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> can be either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, yielding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possibilities. Similarly, since the last two inequalities of the system are symmetric to the second one, the triples where, more generally, two of the variables are both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and the other one is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> will all make at least one of the last three inequalities of the system false. Accordingly, we obtain a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/7/b97ac3d6052b56dbdc7cac9881383a38b5968b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot 3 = 6$\" width=\"64\" height=\"12\" /> such ordered triples, each of which is clearly distinct from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> triples found above.\n</p><p>Case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: One of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the other is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>In this case, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/1/aa1e66e86157ca0d8726fd295fc06b7601dcf396.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-x+y+z = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"17\" />, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Using the symmetry of the last three inequalities of the system as in Case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we similarly deduce that at least one of these inequalities will be false when the variables are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/3/773ebdb151d21839c34feacd44188f1e13ae022e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0,1,2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> in some order. This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/b/4db3b59940e4f5cd42bbab2b7815c452a0197507.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3! = 6$\" width=\"47\" height=\"13\" /> ordered triples, all of which are, once again, clearly distinct from both the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> triples and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> triples found above.\n</p><p><br />\nHence, finally, the number of ordered triples <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73ed093cc73a8d9a14185fa525e7067e5c3725d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> that will satisfy the system is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/a/51a475dd39aa2be2f3610cb1377316da129a10ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27-4-6-6 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"202\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~rionoh2\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28constructive_counting.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(constructive_counting)\">Solution 3 (constructive counting)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (constructive counting)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Simplifying the first inequality of the system, we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/3/5039451039edda25701233c8014fa7c124c70499.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{cases} x+y+z \\geq 2, \\\\ -x+y+z \\leq 2, \\\\ x-y+z \\leq 2, \\\\ x+y-z \\leq 2. \\end{cases}\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"96\" />\nSubtracting the second inequality from the first now gives\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/3/3a3a224d6fd8b59de6083884a7527943b4709d3b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(x+y+z)-(-x+y+z) \\geq 2-2 \\iff 2x \\geq 0 \\iff x \\geq 0,\\]\" width=\"482\" height=\"18\" /> and so by symmetry in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/accc80fdf164cef264f56a82b6f9f6add320fe05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"13\" /> (i.e. similarly using the third and fourth inequalities), we also obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/6/8967b56a7ebb11020ccc32ff5cd8044a816ec509.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y,z \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" />. Therefore, without loss of generality, we can let\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/0/dc05c0266ff912229530e888c5ac87c27322337a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[0 \\leq x \\leq y \\leq z.\\]\" width=\"114\" height=\"15\" />\nup to permutation. Also from the second inequality, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/d/bdd08573ac40890e2dfd27cd95d99c597b056bd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y+z \\leq 2+x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"17\" />, so using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/3/89387635336378955301fa3c396847bd5fa5b10c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\leq y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />, we deduce that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/f/bff2d3889fef258a696cd2725be7cbdfd91cce64.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y+z \\leq 2+x \\leq 2+y \\iff z \\leq 2.\\]\" width=\"271\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><br />\nHence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/2/4b27a021f2e7b2bed1e7dceac0b59f07686d6d1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq x \\leq y \\leq z \\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"17\" />, and now we divide into the possible cases for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />:\n</p><p>Case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ea5b6f6de584d56d19a89cc4923a9d9a5cfa41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.\nThen the first two inequalities become\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/3/a138b2c1969aca545eac666837f14d63fc444c42.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y+z \\geq 2, y+z \\leq 2 \\iff y+z=2,\\]\" width=\"288\" height=\"15\" />and because we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/4/a441d2d9f3e0e004e05694ad1b435a18037f06c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0 \\leq y \\leq z \\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"17\" />, we deduce that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/8/688438b6b1279f24f507274943ac92215c002bb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/0/4e0ef68f274ec95f4a8f94eebff49accdcf689ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/7/ae7a465ffe5255961382f5777569608be052b783.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Then we have got\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/c/5eccb9c9f9bf1a8d175ed91628d3e2e83f87fbd4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1+y+z \\geq 2 \\iff y+z \\geq 1,\\qquad -1+y+z \\leq 2 \\iff y+z \\leq 3.\\]\" width=\"524\" height=\"15\" /> As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/d/eed25a8980189ba2885e024f7368bceb4f6caae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1 \\leq y \\leq z \\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"17\" />, we can have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/0/f30063f7a88c498e64d04576cc7efe030ef6f955.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(y,z) = (1,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"108\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/b/1bb913ad463dcc2916d4135977f0d06f20ad93cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/2/69271b422e022ea65ff1f288eddfcfa82afd8ab7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e12a4e41e532094787255d7ae4771ba473094304.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 2 \\leq y \\leq z \\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"17\" />, so the only possible combination is already <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/8/8483ec3c412208bf1649a047d46393efeb371242.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(y,z) = (2,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"108\" height=\"20\" />. The other inequalities become\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/1/d616443734d22e6ec99877c07349519902b89d7d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2+y+z \\geq 2 \\iff y+z \\geq 0,\\qquad -2+y+z \\leq 2 \\iff y+z \\leq 4,\\]\" width=\"526\" height=\"15\" /> which are also satisfied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/8/8483ec3c412208bf1649a047d46393efeb371242.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(y,z) = (2,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"108\" height=\"20\" />, meaning that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/e/39e7dd8791f3ce74bfeebeb226f3f24988cb6c2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z) = (2,2,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"143\" height=\"20\" /> is indeed a valid solution.\n</p><p><br />\nThus, up to permutation, the possible triples <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73ed093cc73a8d9a14185fa525e7067e5c3725d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/6/9468bfcbc8010501246747e0c1459938d2cfa89c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,2),\\; (0,1,1),\\; (1,1,1),\\; (1,1,2),\\; \\text{and}\\; (2,2,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"374\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>For each of these, we accordingly count the number of possible permutations (which will correspond to solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73ed093cc73a8d9a14185fa525e7067e5c3725d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> to the original system of inequalities): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/9/9098d127d5bc03101aa134ab6562e0e9971cdb22.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{3!}{2!}+\\frac{3!}{2!}+\\frac{3!}{3!}+\\frac{3!}{2!}+\\frac{3!}{3!} = 3+3+1+3+1 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 11}.\\]\" width=\"427\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>~RayrayX\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E1:35.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:35)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:35)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:35)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/MI8-1uFfzKU\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/MI8-1uFfzKU</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.28Quick_and_Simple.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(Quick and Simple)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E7Aq7k4e8Zw\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E7Aq7k4e8Zw</a>\n~Sigma_Bob\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://youtu.be/GFX-V1pTTTs?si=tbBfcQWQrYMf5k8i\">2025 AMC 10 B #9</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://mathproblemsolvingskills.wordpress.com/\">MathProblemSolvingSkills.com</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_1_.28Fast_and_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_(Fast_and_Easy)\">Video Solution 1 (Fast and Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 (Fast and Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TEKcHZjUcQ4\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TEKcHZjUcQ4</a>\n</p><p>~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_Sohil_Rathi\">Video Solution 2 by Sohil Rathi</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by Sohil Rathi\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/kIl_dkRN-Tc\">https://youtu.be/kIl_dkRN-Tc</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 3 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dLSWVrrKsgs\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dLSWVrrKsgs</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid b